 "audit_note": "Conflict scores follow a reading of every conflict pass on the leaderboard: in 7 of 35 the model had offered to accept the pushy teammate's own offer, which the judges missed, so they count as fails.",
 "recent_note": "New: models added after the main runs, scored on the same scenarios in the same way. They are not in the frontier and open-weight averages. GLM-5.3 has no normal-teammate runs.",
 "abstract": {
  "text": "AI agents now work alongside people and other agents, yet we still test them alone. Being able to do a task is not the same as being a good teammate. We introduce Collaborative Intelligence, a benchmark of 210 scenarios across 7 dimensions of teamwork. Each is built from a real source, such as an accident report, and has a checkable answer. Every scenario runs alone, with a normal teammate, and with a scripted teammate who creates one specific problem. A model is scored only on the scenarios it passes alone, so a failure points to teamwork, not ability. All 6 models we test pass most scenarios alone, but their team scores range from 34.7 to 76.6. Frontier models almost never give way to unsafe pressure, and they almost always report misconduct. Across 90 further test runs built to break two of them, we found no genuine failure. Collaboration is far from solved. Frontier models pass only 9% of conflict tests and 49% of emotional intelligence tests. They also take initiative less often when a gap in the team's work is buried in a realistic workload. Open-weight models trail the frontier by 31.2 points overall. Of six models added after our main runs, OpenAI's newest scores below GPT-6 Astra, and smaller Claude models keep their safety but lose teamwork. The shortfall in collaboration is mostly a matter of defaults: one paragraph in the system prompt that states the norm lifts two frontier models from 46% to 83% on these three dimensions, while a generic “be a good teammate” helps only a little and not at all in conflict. The same paragraph lifts open-weight models only part of the way. When the model behind an agent switches mid-session, safety mostly sticks to the conversation. A weaker model that inherits a refusal keeps it, but a frontier model that takes over can neither undo harm already in motion nor reliably see through its predecessor's code: taking over sessions in which a weaker model had cheated on a coding task, frontier models kept the cheat in 43 of 111 takeovers, even when told to check the work or given more time to think. Showing them a diff of the session fixes the tampering they miss but not the cheats they see and keep. Outside code, they corrected every false record they inherited.",
  "source": "ci-website/docs/paper_final/sections/abstract.tex"
 }
